# Notification banners

A banner carries a service-wide notice or an outcome from the previous step.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## Scope the notice before choosing a variant

Use the [message decision guide](status.html#choose-the-right-message) to distinguish a notice from a state label or unread count. This page owns the ordinary notice and confirmed-outcome contract. The [global-banner source variant](../publishing/components/global-banner.html) adds service-wide scope and dismissal-lifecycle questions. The [urgent-message source variant](../publishing/components/emergency-banner.html) requires a verified urgency rule and fresh source context; it does not make a maintenance notice urgent.

A source variation changes context, not the evidence needed to claim success. Keep inline validation with its field and preserve the error summary when a form cannot be submitted.

## When to use it

Use an information banner for planned downtime affecting the dashboard, or a success banner after a completed settings save on the previous page. Prioritise one coherent notice.

## When not to use it

Do not place field validation errors in a banner. Do not let a success message compete with an error summary. Put information directly relevant to the current task in the main content.

## Home Assistant adaptation

State what is affected and when it applies. A temporary integration outage is not evidence that a physical device is off. Success wording must reflect the result confirmed by Home Assistant, not merely that a button was pressed. Remove stale success notices after navigation.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Give a static banner a labelled region and a meaningful heading. For a dynamically inserted result, choose an appropriate one-time announcement and avoid repeated alerts from polling. Do not move focus for routine background status updates.

